## Communication style

- **Lead with effect for the operator, not internals** ("logins survive restarts"); mechanics only if asked or decision-relevant.
- **Every decision uses the briefing format:** (1) decision and why (1 plain sentence); (2) <=3 options + 1-line trade-off; (3) pick + why; (4) do-nothing cost.
  - Verify options execute first: run `--dry-run`/read-only and read the answer (refusing scripts aren't options); give exact steps for user-only ones.
  - Code/system changes: measure impact before the card on today's data (never memory/prior reply): fault, outcome when firing, code/tests touched, readers, deploy, and what stops (offer process restart before machine restart).
  - Speed options: time prototype sequentially (old then new), 3 runs each, median + load (profile share isn't an outcome).
- **Report done work checkably without guessing:** what changed, practical effect, how to see it working.
- **Write deliverables for their audience, not the operator:** open with reader, unknowns (define internal tools like gearbox in 1 line), and why it matters; readable on first pass without knowing the operator.
